#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
r"""
HTA Bridge  -  extract, document and drive the Hybrid Test Application (HTA)
=============================================================================

WHAT IT DOES
------------
1. EXTRACT   every HTA widget (tab, name, type, min/max limits, step, unit,
             button states, simulation-model path, position, size) into an
             Excel workbook - one sheet per HTA tab + an index + a Sequence sheet.
             Sources (combine any of them, results are merged):
               * HTA layout / view-set XML files      -> fast & complete (recommended)
               * live scan of the running HTA (UI Automation, every tab)
               * HTA "Change Widget Properties" dialogs (right-click -> Properties)
2. CONTROL   load that workbook in the GUI, type a value against any name and
             it is pushed into the running HTA: range-checked against the
             limits, read back & verified, logged to a CSV audit trail.
3. AUTOMATE  run test steps (SET / CHECK / WAIT / WAIT_UNTIL / PRESS / READ /
             PAUSE / LOG) from the "Sequence" sheet -> PASS/FAIL Excel report.

INSTALL  (Windows 10/11, Python 3.9+)
-------------------------------------
    pip install openpyxl pywinauto PySide6 psutil lxml
    (lxml and psutil are optional but recommended)

QUICK START
-----------
    python hta_bridge.py demo                     # offline demo (simulated HTA)
    python hta_bridge.py find-layouts             # locate HTA layout XML files
    python hta_bridge.py extract --xml "C:\HTA\Layouts" --live -o HTA_Signals.xlsx
    python hta_bridge.py gui HTA_Signals.xlsx     # GUI -> HTA menu -> Connect HTA
    python hta_bridge.py set HTA_Signals.xlsx "System/Ignition OnOff" On
    python hta_bridge.py apply HTA_Signals.xlsx   # push every 'Set Value' cell
    python hta_bridge.py run HTA_Signals.xlsx     # run the Sequence sheet
    python hta_bridge.py inspect                  # dump HTA control tree (support)
    python hta_bridge.py selftest                 # built-in test suite

HOW VALUES REACH HTA
--------------------
HTA is driven exactly like a user would drive it, through Windows UI
Automation (pywinauto): select the tab, locate the widget (automation id ->
paired caption label -> XML position), type the value + Enter (or use the UIA
Value/RangeValue pattern), click toggle buttons until they show the wanted
state, then read the control back to verify.  Nothing is written to the
simulation model behind HTA's back, so HTA's own limits/logic still apply.

All files the tool creates live in  %USERPROFILE%\HTA_Bridge  (settings, logs,
audit trail, sequence reports) unless configured otherwise.
"""
